If `n` is zero or negative, the outer loop does not run, so nothing is printed.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the total number of printed stars is `n + (n - 1) + ... + 1`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output.

Instead of printing each star separately, this keeps a string for the current row. After printing it, one character is removed before the next row.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern5(int n) {
        string currentRow(n, '*');

        while (!currentRow.empty()) {
            cout << currentRow << endl;
            currentRow.pop_back();
        }
    }
};
```

The first string has `n` stars. Removing its last character gives the next shorter row, so the output keeps the correct order.

